### NOTE (3.2.0, toolchain 6.4.66 -> 6.5.3): the level shift above is the HOST, not the bump.
### Every figure in the v3.2.0 block is ~1.5-3.7x the v3.1.4 block. Controlled A/B on this same
### machine, same hour, identical bench source, `cyrius build --no-deps` + direct run:
###   A  cycc 6.4.66 + stdlib 6.4.66  (the exact 3.1.4 world)
###   B  cycc 6.5.3  + stdlib 6.5.3   (the 3.2.0 world)
###   C  cycc 6.5.3  + stdlib 6.4.66  (compiler isolated from stdlib)
###                              A(old)     B(new)     C(mix)
###   dispatch_initialize        2.924us    2.864us    2.901us
###   dispatch_tools_call        7.266us    6.701us    6.972us
###   jsonx_get_str_flat         1.474us    1.475us    1.474us
###   codec_process_message      9.103us    8.809us    9.203us
###   schema_compile_nested      7.568us    7.662us    7.516us
###   auth_bearer_check_unset    1.347us    1.333us    1.347us
###   auth_bearer_check_set      2.140us    2.124us    2.137us
### A == B == C within run-to-run noise, and the 6.4.66 control reproduces the SLOW numbers.
### So the bump is performance-neutral; the host regressed between 2026-07-17 and 2026-07-30.
### bench.cyr and chrono.cyr are byte-identical between the two snapshots (diff -q, rc=0), so it
### is not a measurement-harness change either. Compare v3.2.0 forward against v3.2.0, not
### against v3.1.4, until the host baseline is re-established.

